                <pb facs="0168" n="64" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg64"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3637: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai (Cunningham) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-25T18:00:00+08:00">January 25, 1932—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>This Consulate General has been informed by Eugene
                        Chen that both he and Sun
                        Fo have resigned and that Chiang
                        Kai-shek and Wang Ching-wei
                    are in control at Nanking. He resigned because he claims that Chiang Kai-shek insists on following a passive
                    policy. Had he remained in office Mr. Chen intended to implement article 16 of the League
                    Covenant.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cunningham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
